WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:17.220 --> 00:00:20.770
Some people they just got
fired and they had no idea why.

00:00:20.770 --> 00:00:23.670
Some people were detained,
losing access to healthcare.

00:00:23.670 --> 00:00:25.910
How many have been detained

00:00:25.910 --> 00:00:28.530
as prisoners in their own country?

00:00:28.530 --> 00:00:30.210
How many have denied pensions?

00:00:30.210 --> 00:00:32.090
How many have lost their job?

00:00:32.090 --> 00:00:34.757
This is a day of national shame.

00:00:36.730 --> 00:00:39.200
The Windrush scandal
almost embodies in a way

00:00:39.200 --> 00:00:40.810
for some people, how Black people are seen

00:00:40.810 --> 00:00:41.950
and treated in the U.K.

00:00:41.950 --> 00:00:42.910
because it's this example

00:00:42.910 --> 00:00:44.330
where people who did all the right things

00:00:44.330 --> 00:00:46.340
suddenly had a huge amount of their rights

00:00:46.340 --> 00:00:47.630
and their access to
things that they'd always

00:00:47.630 --> 00:00:49.350
taken for granted just
taken away from them

00:00:49.350 --> 00:00:51.510
without any real explanation.

00:00:51.510 --> 00:00:53.110
It really becomes this question of,

00:00:53.110 --> 00:00:55.730
was that just something
that was a series of really,

00:00:55.730 --> 00:00:57.010
really unfortunate mistakes?

00:00:57.010 --> 00:00:58.230
Or is there something at play where

00:00:58.230 --> 00:01:00.090
it is because the vast majority

00:01:00.090 --> 00:01:01.890
of the Windrush generation is Black?

00:01:03.300 --> 00:01:05.840
The Motherland, that's
what Britain was called,

00:01:05.840 --> 00:01:07.934
the Motherland

00:01:07.934 --> 00:01:10.500
and then the mother just
rejected her children.

00:01:10.500 --> 00:01:12.120
For what reason?

00:01:12.120 --> 00:01:14.013
We didn't do anything wrong.

00:01:19.512 --> 00:01:20.345
Here sees the arrival

00:01:20.345 --> 00:01:22.470
of more than 400 happy Jamaicans.

00:01:22.470 --> 00:01:24.400
They've come to seek work in Britain

00:01:24.400 --> 00:01:26.440
and are ready and willing
to do any kind of job

00:01:26.440 --> 00:01:28.950
that will help the Motherland
along the road to prosperity.

00:01:28.950 --> 00:01:30.570
So the Empire Windrush was a ship

00:01:30.570 --> 00:01:32.900
that actually came to England in 1948

00:01:32.900 --> 00:01:35.340
and most of its passengers
were from the Caribbean

00:01:35.340 --> 00:01:36.710
and they were all coming to England

00:01:36.710 --> 00:01:38.860
to live and work across the U.K.

00:01:38.860 --> 00:01:41.470
and it's become synonymous
with an entire generation

00:01:41.470 --> 00:01:44.400
of people that came over about 25 years

00:01:44.400 --> 00:01:46.490
because it was people who came from,

00:01:46.490 --> 00:01:48.810
at the time what was colonies
in the British Empire,

00:01:48.810 --> 00:01:52.440
to the U.K. to live in England
as citizens of that empire.

00:01:52.440 --> 00:01:55.020
In 1954 about 10,000 West Indians...

00:01:55.020 --> 00:01:56.520
The Windrush generation

00:01:56.520 --> 00:01:59.003
embodies a pivotal moment
in British history.

00:02:00.130 --> 00:02:01.990
Their arrival would set a new course

00:02:01.990 --> 00:02:04.840
for the nation toward multiculturalism.

00:02:04.840 --> 00:02:07.630
But to better understand
issues around race today,

00:02:07.630 --> 00:02:09.750
we have to look to Britain's past.

00:02:09.750 --> 00:02:13.010
Specifically, the British Empire.

00:02:13.010 --> 00:02:15.890
In the 1600s, England colonized much of

00:02:15.890 --> 00:02:18.130
what it is now places like Jamaica

00:02:18.130 --> 00:02:20.880
and through over hundreds of years

00:02:20.880 --> 00:02:23.150
developed this trade
network, which involved

00:02:23.150 --> 00:02:25.970
taking millions of people from Africa

00:02:25.970 --> 00:02:28.020
and then taking them to the Caribbean

00:02:28.020 --> 00:02:30.370
and then forcing them to
work on slave plantations

00:02:30.370 --> 00:02:32.480
where conditions were horrific.

00:02:32.480 --> 00:02:35.853
But it was a crucial part of
the British Empire's economy.

00:02:37.000 --> 00:02:39.980
When British slavery ended in the 1830s

00:02:39.980 --> 00:02:43.600
there was no real investment
in the colonized countries.

00:02:43.600 --> 00:02:46.630
The formerly enslaved Africans
who lived in these colonies

00:02:46.630 --> 00:02:48.900
essentially became British citizens

00:02:48.900 --> 00:02:52.120
and began to migrate around
the empire to find work.

00:02:53.090 --> 00:02:55.330
Around the beginning of the 20th century,

00:02:55.330 --> 00:02:57.140
the United Kingdom began to restrict

00:02:57.140 --> 00:02:59.990
its immigration policies,
severely impacting

00:02:59.990 --> 00:03:02.303
how many people outside
Britain could earn.

00:03:03.960 --> 00:03:06.070
But when the Motherland needed labor

00:03:06.070 --> 00:03:07.923
it put out a call for workers.

00:03:08.800 --> 00:03:10.560
Britain needed people to rebuild

00:03:10.560 --> 00:03:12.310
after World War II, after being decimated.

00:03:12.310 --> 00:03:14.160
They're all full of hope for the future.

00:03:14.160 --> 00:03:15.700
So let's make them very welcome

00:03:15.700 --> 00:03:17.803
as they begin their new life over here.

00:03:18.860 --> 00:03:20.640
Hundreds of thousands of people would come

00:03:20.640 --> 00:03:24.350
from former British colonies to the U.K.

00:03:24.350 --> 00:03:26.750
and that seems like a
reasonable step to take

00:03:26.750 --> 00:03:29.140
because they were part
of this global empire

00:03:29.140 --> 00:03:32.460
and because they had this
connection to England.

00:03:32.460 --> 00:03:33.780
When the Empire Windrush arrived

00:03:33.780 --> 00:03:35.790
I think a calypso singer
was asked to sing the song,

00:03:35.790 --> 00:03:37.997
which was like "London
is the Place for Me."

00:03:37.997 --> 00:03:42.997
♪ London is the place for me ♪

00:03:43.223 --> 00:03:47.137
♪ London, this lovely city ♪

00:03:47.137 --> 00:03:49.240
And it was about the beauty of the city

00:03:49.240 --> 00:03:50.860
and about settling down there

00:03:50.860 --> 00:03:53.155
and just the excitement
of being in London.

00:03:53.155 --> 00:03:55.390
Now the end of their journey is near.

00:03:55.390 --> 00:03:56.680
What will they find in the land

00:03:56.680 --> 00:03:58.133
they regard as an El Dorado?

00:03:59.030 --> 00:04:01.010
But then the experiences of some people,

00:04:01.010 --> 00:04:03.060
obviously wasn't great.

00:04:03.060 --> 00:04:06.400
Some White people refused to
work besides Black people.

00:04:06.400 --> 00:04:08.240
Some people were refused jobs.

00:04:08.240 --> 00:04:09.700
Some people were sent to live

00:04:09.700 --> 00:04:13.040
in accommodation that was horrible.

00:04:13.040 --> 00:04:14.410
There is this big discrepancy

00:04:14.410 --> 00:04:16.040
between how people see themselves

00:04:16.040 --> 00:04:17.930
who are coming from places like Jamaica,

00:04:17.930 --> 00:04:20.193
to how people saw them coming in.

00:04:21.150 --> 00:04:22.870
There was a real kind of hostility amongst

00:04:22.870 --> 00:04:25.100
some parts of the population
to these new arrivals

00:04:25.100 --> 00:04:28.603
and it was in some way specifically
because they were Black.

00:04:32.490 --> 00:04:34.570
My name's Glenda Caesar.

00:04:34.570 --> 00:04:37.823
I'm a Windrush campaigner/victim.

00:04:38.840 --> 00:04:42.573
I came to the U.K. when
I was three months old.

00:04:43.450 --> 00:04:45.383
Both my parents are from Dominica.

00:04:46.320 --> 00:04:50.580
Mummy and Daddy came over in the '60s

00:04:50.580 --> 00:04:53.030
and they came over for a better life.

00:04:53.030 --> 00:04:53.870
You know?

00:04:53.870 --> 00:04:58.410
They wanted their own home
in the Motherland, England.

00:04:58.410 --> 00:05:02.960
They were told that the
streets were paved with gold.

00:05:02.960 --> 00:05:06.870
They respected England,
England was everything to them.

00:05:06.870 --> 00:05:09.080
[Interviewer] And how did you
find growing up in London?

00:05:09.080 --> 00:05:10.640
Oh, I loved it.

00:05:10.640 --> 00:05:13.180
Our house was right in
the middle of the road,

00:05:13.180 --> 00:05:15.160
so it was so diverse

00:05:15.160 --> 00:05:18.540
because there was families
from every country.

00:05:18.540 --> 00:05:22.450
We had Indian family,
family from Pakistan,

00:05:22.450 --> 00:05:25.290
a family from Bangladesh, Mauritians,

00:05:25.290 --> 00:05:27.910
St. Vincent, English, Scottish, Irish.

00:05:27.910 --> 00:05:29.440
It was so diverse.

00:05:29.440 --> 00:05:30.810
[Interviewer] And if anyone had asked you

00:05:30.810 --> 00:05:32.440
when you were growing
up where you were from,

00:05:32.440 --> 00:05:33.980
what would you have said?

00:05:33.980 --> 00:05:35.980
England, I'm British.

00:05:35.980 --> 00:05:37.913
I had never been to Dominica.

00:05:38.860 --> 00:05:43.860
I didn't know something was
wrong until after my mum went

00:05:44.310 --> 00:05:47.550
to Dominica on holiday, and
my mum fell sick out there,

00:05:47.550 --> 00:05:50.940
and I wanted to go out
there and be with my mum.

00:05:50.940 --> 00:05:55.420
I made an application
for a British passport

00:05:55.420 --> 00:05:58.340
and that's when I found
out that they said,

00:05:58.340 --> 00:05:59.930
"No, you're not British.

00:05:59.930 --> 00:06:01.440
If you leave this country again,

00:06:01.440 --> 00:06:02.960
you won't be able to come back."

00:06:02.960 --> 00:06:06.030
And I was just getting
told "You're not British."

00:06:06.030 --> 00:06:09.346
I had my youngest son in 1988

00:06:09.346 --> 00:06:11.679
and that's when I found out

00:06:11.679 --> 00:06:13.860
that he wasn't British either.

00:06:13.860 --> 00:06:16.073
Within that time my mum passed away.

00:06:19.210 --> 00:06:22.830
Yeah, and I, I just,

00:06:22.830 --> 00:06:26.000
I couldn't get out
there to be with my mum.

00:06:26.000 --> 00:06:28.120
We want to ensure that only legal migrants

00:06:28.120 --> 00:06:30.870
have access to the labor
market, free health services,

00:06:30.870 --> 00:06:33.440
housing, bank accounts
and driving licenses

00:06:33.440 --> 00:06:35.470
and this is not just about making the U.K.

00:06:35.470 --> 00:06:38.000
a more hostile place for illegal migrants,

00:06:38.000 --> 00:06:40.483
it is also about fairness.

00:06:42.820 --> 00:06:44.760
So in 2012, there's this issue

00:06:44.760 --> 00:06:46.400
of immigration coming up again

00:06:46.400 --> 00:06:48.090
and there's this real sense that

00:06:48.090 --> 00:06:50.390
Britain doesn't have
control over its borders.

00:06:51.680 --> 00:06:52.970
The British government enforced

00:06:52.970 --> 00:06:56.133
an immigration policy known
as the 'hostile environment'.

00:06:57.100 --> 00:06:58.775
The message was clear:

00:06:58.775 --> 00:07:00.083
If you're here illegally,

00:07:00.083 --> 00:07:01.545
pack your bags and leave.

00:07:02.430 --> 00:07:04.160
But the hostile environment swept up

00:07:04.160 --> 00:07:06.000
more than illegal immigrants.

00:07:06.000 --> 00:07:07.680
Citizens of the British Commonwealth

00:07:07.680 --> 00:07:09.620
were caught in the dragnet

00:07:09.620 --> 00:07:11.823
and it turned their lives upside down.

00:07:12.780 --> 00:07:15.996
I retired and I took a part-time position

00:07:15.996 --> 00:07:18.712
in 2009

00:07:18.712 --> 00:07:21.610
and it was then that it
started getting complicated

00:07:21.610 --> 00:07:25.110
because the employer
wanted more documentation

00:07:25.110 --> 00:07:27.420
from me to say that I was British

00:07:27.420 --> 00:07:29.863
and I was like, well,
I've got a work history.

00:07:30.720 --> 00:07:32.290
You can go anywhere,

00:07:32.290 --> 00:07:34.560
I've got my children's birth
certificate, all British.

00:07:34.560 --> 00:07:36.130
They're all British, you can check.

00:07:36.130 --> 00:07:37.560
But that wasn't good enough

00:07:37.560 --> 00:07:41.950
and I got terminated from
my part-time position

00:07:41.950 --> 00:07:43.440
as a GP administrator

00:07:43.440 --> 00:07:46.510
because I didn't have
the right to work here

00:07:46.510 --> 00:07:48.560
and that's when I realized it was

00:07:48.560 --> 00:07:50.301
really, really serious.

00:07:50.301 --> 00:07:51.726
Really serious.

00:07:52.660 --> 00:07:54.920
I got into debt, a lot of debt.

00:07:54.920 --> 00:07:56.563
I nearly lost my home.

00:07:57.400 --> 00:07:59.150
I was in court many times

00:07:59.150 --> 00:08:01.830
trying to save the roof over my head.

00:08:01.830 --> 00:08:04.140
So I started selling old trainers

00:08:04.140 --> 00:08:07.360
so that I wouldn't have to ask my children

00:08:07.360 --> 00:08:11.150
to help me financially, top up
the gas, top up the electric,

00:08:11.150 --> 00:08:12.930
I need some food.

00:08:12.930 --> 00:08:17.000
And even with my daughter,
I had to share her benefits,

00:08:17.000 --> 00:08:19.075
her disability benefits.

00:08:19.075 --> 00:08:22.157
But it was embarrassing sometimes

00:08:22.157 --> 00:08:24.150
because sometimes you
get to a position where

00:08:24.150 --> 00:08:26.080
you wanna buy something and you thought,

00:08:26.080 --> 00:08:27.262
God, I've gotta ask them

00:08:27.262 --> 00:08:29.950
and they've got their
children to maintain as well.

00:08:29.950 --> 00:08:32.790
My son got depressed and
I got depressed as well.

00:08:32.790 --> 00:08:34.761
I started blaming myself

00:08:34.761 --> 00:08:39.520
and there was a time where
I contemplated suicide.

00:08:39.520 --> 00:08:43.000
But in my head I'm thinking, if I do that,

00:08:43.000 --> 00:08:45.060
then what's gonna happen to the grandkids?

00:08:45.060 --> 00:08:47.510
And then how's he gonna
get out of the situation?

00:08:49.630 --> 00:08:52.910
Since 1971, it'd been U.K. law that anyone

00:08:52.910 --> 00:08:56.230
who'd come from Commonwealth
countries before 1973,

00:08:56.230 --> 00:08:59.143
like Glenda's parents
from Dominica, could stay.

00:09:00.180 --> 00:09:01.860
But many weren't given documentation

00:09:01.860 --> 00:09:04.210
to prove when they'd arrived.

00:09:04.210 --> 00:09:06.010
This hadn't been an issue until

00:09:06.010 --> 00:09:08.193
the 'hostile environment'
came into effect.

00:09:10.000 --> 00:09:11.660
To make matters worse,

00:09:11.660 --> 00:09:14.040
the government department
in charge of immigration

00:09:14.040 --> 00:09:15.980
destroyed the landing cards of those

00:09:15.980 --> 00:09:20.020
that came between 1948 and 1973.

00:09:20.020 --> 00:09:22.180
Those landing cards could've
been used potentially

00:09:22.180 --> 00:09:23.890
as a way of the Windrush generation

00:09:23.890 --> 00:09:25.230
proving their right to be in the country.

00:09:25.230 --> 00:09:26.970
So the fact is, is that
we don't actually know

00:09:26.970 --> 00:09:28.788
how many people were caught up in it.

00:09:28.788 --> 00:09:30.260
It's safe to say thousands,

00:09:30.260 --> 00:09:33.213
but it's sad, but some people
have died without us knowing.

00:09:34.540 --> 00:09:36.710
Glenda fought for the
best part of a decade

00:09:36.710 --> 00:09:38.343
to prove she was a citizen.

00:09:39.780 --> 00:09:42.070
Even though in my head I was British,

00:09:42.070 --> 00:09:43.920
I thought maybe I haven't done enough.

00:09:43.920 --> 00:09:44.753
Is that a smile?

00:09:44.753 --> 00:09:46.119
It is a smile.

00:09:46.119 --> 00:09:47.127
What happened?

00:09:47.127 --> 00:09:49.202
I've been, they've told me

00:09:49.202 --> 00:09:51.450
because I came before 1971

00:09:51.450 --> 00:09:53.250
I should get my British citizenship.

00:09:53.250 --> 00:09:54.730
They called my son's name and said,

00:09:54.730 --> 00:09:56.800
oh no, he's got his citizenship

00:09:56.800 --> 00:10:01.541
and I burst out crying because

00:10:01.541 --> 00:10:05.041
the journey wasn't about me,

00:10:05.041 --> 00:10:06.757
it was about my son,

00:10:06.757 --> 00:10:08.323
him belonging.

00:10:12.240 --> 00:10:13.073
Sorry.

00:10:15.040 --> 00:10:17.180
Morning, Home Secretary.
Morning.

00:10:17.180 --> 00:10:18.020
When will your government

00:10:18.020 --> 00:10:20.290
get a grip on the Windrush crisis?

00:10:20.290 --> 00:10:22.560
The U.K. government
acknowledged its mistake

00:10:22.560 --> 00:10:24.500
and undertook a compensation program

00:10:24.500 --> 00:10:28.480
for victims of what became
known as the Windrush scandal.

00:10:28.480 --> 00:10:31.900
There was an opportunity
to right the wrongs done

00:10:31.900 --> 00:10:36.170
by past governments and to
offer payment after apologizing

00:10:36.170 --> 00:10:38.250
to that generation for
what they'd suffered.

00:10:38.250 --> 00:10:41.690
But the reality was
significantly more complicated.

00:10:41.690 --> 00:10:44.280
For Glenda and so many others like her,

00:10:44.280 --> 00:10:46.100
the hostile environment wouldn't end

00:10:46.100 --> 00:10:47.700
when they got their citizenship.

00:10:48.620 --> 00:10:51.470
The U.K. government offered her £22,000

00:10:51.470 --> 00:10:55.560
for loss of employment, being
detained, and impact on life.

00:10:55.560 --> 00:10:59.850
22,000 for what, nearly nine to 10 years

00:10:59.850 --> 00:11:01.925
of loss of everything?

00:11:01.925 --> 00:11:03.010
No way.

00:11:03.010 --> 00:11:05.570
I was so mad, I was fuming.

00:11:05.570 --> 00:11:07.110
That didn't even cover my wages.

00:11:07.110 --> 00:11:10.060
That wouldn't even have covered
the unemployment benefit

00:11:10.060 --> 00:11:12.660
if I was entitled to it, for those years.

00:11:12.660 --> 00:11:15.393
I always say it's like a burglar,

00:11:15.393 --> 00:11:16.685
burgling your house

00:11:16.685 --> 00:11:18.443
and then you have to go and

00:11:18.443 --> 00:11:21.240
beg this burglar to give
you back your stuff.

00:11:21.240 --> 00:11:24.416
On average, people were
receiving less than £10,000

00:11:24.416 --> 00:11:26.163
as part of the payment.

00:11:27.500 --> 00:11:29.070
When people had been
out of work for years.

00:11:29.070 --> 00:11:31.610
So imagine how many, how
much money you have lost

00:11:31.610 --> 00:11:34.320
if you haven't been able
to work for say five years.

00:11:34.320 --> 00:11:36.490
There are many that will
criticize the government

00:11:36.490 --> 00:11:38.760
for how it arrived at the numbers.

00:11:38.760 --> 00:11:42.380
But in the tone and in the difficulty

00:11:42.380 --> 00:11:45.830
and in the lack of belief
of those who have suffered,

00:11:45.830 --> 00:11:49.850
in turning the burden on them to prove

00:11:49.850 --> 00:11:53.380
that they have been systemically abused

00:11:53.380 --> 00:11:55.380
and then not being satisfied

00:11:55.380 --> 00:11:58.800
with their paper trail, or their answers.

00:11:58.800 --> 00:12:01.500
I think we really learned

00:12:01.500 --> 00:12:05.210
that neither the substantive redress,

00:12:05.210 --> 00:12:10.210
nor its delivery, has been acceptable.

00:12:10.780 --> 00:12:15.620
There continues to be
major concerns amongst

00:12:15.620 --> 00:12:18.980
the community of survivors
and those that support them

00:12:18.980 --> 00:12:22.740
that the scheme will
ever be fit for purpose

00:12:22.740 --> 00:12:27.200
and that's because some of
the tweaking that has happened

00:12:27.200 --> 00:12:30.338
hasn't fundamentally altered the fact

00:12:30.338 --> 00:12:35.338
that it is a scheme that
remains lacking in impartiality,

00:12:35.338 --> 00:12:38.370
and really gives the sense of

00:12:38.370 --> 00:12:41.490
the government marking its own homework.

00:12:41.490 --> 00:12:46.490
Now that is a government
that many will accept

00:12:46.760 --> 00:12:50.640
is guilty of institutional racism

00:12:50.640 --> 00:12:54.050
and so really, justice
must not only be done,

00:12:54.050 --> 00:12:55.943
but it must be seen to be done.

00:12:57.240 --> 00:13:00.010
We have set up a unit, which
is helping those people

00:13:00.010 --> 00:13:02.650
to get the documents that they need.

00:13:02.650 --> 00:13:05.120
They are British, they are part of us.

00:13:05.120 --> 00:13:06.560
I think you have to say that actually

00:13:06.560 --> 00:13:09.120
there's a lot more lessons to learn.

00:13:09.120 --> 00:13:12.910
But ultimately, we
haven't yet as a society

00:13:12.910 --> 00:13:15.950
and as a country, said sorry in really

00:13:15.950 --> 00:13:17.313
a very meaningful way.

00:13:20.850 --> 00:13:22.810
Last year, after George Floyd's murder,

00:13:22.810 --> 00:13:24.670
we saw protests across the U.K.,

00:13:24.670 --> 00:13:27.300
which in a way mirrored the
ones that we saw in the U.S.

00:13:27.300 --> 00:13:30.800
and a big of that was
about the Windrush scandal.

00:13:30.800 --> 00:13:32.960
The scandal will always be an example

00:13:32.960 --> 00:13:36.070
of a really just blatant
form of discrimination

00:13:36.070 --> 00:13:38.610
and of wrongdoing against people
who were almost all black.

00:13:38.610 --> 00:13:41.540
So it's a question of what do
you do with that information?

00:13:41.540 --> 00:13:43.190
Do you incorporate it
into a larger narrative

00:13:43.190 --> 00:13:45.340
around how Black people are
treated in this country?

00:13:45.340 --> 00:13:47.910
Or do you treat that
as an isolated incident

00:13:47.910 --> 00:13:51.313
that is unfortunate, but
doesn't really engage with race?

00:13:53.530 --> 00:13:55.190
For many, the Windrush scandal

00:13:55.190 --> 00:13:57.980
is symbolic of the U.K.'s
track record on race.

00:13:58.990 --> 00:14:02.210
During the pandemic, 64%
of essential health workers

00:14:02.210 --> 00:14:04.720
who contracted Covid-19 were from Black

00:14:04.720 --> 00:14:07.320
or ethnic minority backgrounds.

00:14:07.320 --> 00:14:08.740
And there's a disproportionate number

00:14:08.740 --> 00:14:10.760
of Black residents in the United Kingdom

00:14:10.760 --> 00:14:12.513
who've died from coronavirus.

00:14:13.770 --> 00:14:17.510
Inequality permeates British society.

00:14:17.510 --> 00:14:20.740
There's still this question
of why do White families

00:14:20.740 --> 00:14:24.163
have nine times that of
a Black African family?

00:14:25.210 --> 00:14:27.230
Is it because the Black family
isn't trying hard enough?

00:14:27.230 --> 00:14:28.410
Is it because there's a single parent?

00:14:28.410 --> 00:14:31.073
Or is it because of
systematic failings at play?

00:14:32.680 --> 00:14:37.561
That, as I say, I think that
conversation is unresolved.

